= Editorial protocol =

{{Source status|status=source-grounded operating method|note=Public-safe distillation of recurring BITwiki research and Book Matter working rules.}}

BITwiki canonicalization follows a research-and-revision process rather than one-pass rewriting.

== Loop ==
<pre>
RAW SIGNAL
→ CONTEXT / SCOPE
→ RESEARCH
→ MULTIPLE LENSES / PARTNERS
→ MODEL / STRUCTURE
→ DISTILL SIGNAL
→ REVISE
→ CROSS-REVIEW
→ VERIFY
→ CANONICALIZE
→ TRANSCLUDE / RELATE
→ RECUR
</pre>

== Rules ==
* Verify state and source before acting.
* Preserve source text before interpreting it.
* Work within explicit scope.
* Use multiple perspectives or reviewers when material is consequential or ambiguous.
* Do not accept the first revision merely because it is coherent.
* Prefer slow, surgical, intelligent small wins over rash multi-component changes.
* Distinguish historical design from current implementation.
* Preserve citations and provenance through moves, merges, rewrites, and transclusion.
* Mark uncertainty, contradiction, speculation, and unresolved source gaps.
* Preserve sophisticated distinctions while removing accidental complexity.
* Optimize for transposability, modularity, composability, depth, nuance, and sophistication.

== Canonicalization is not paraphrasing ==
For important authored passages recover source, date/version, exact wording, later revisions, unique meaning, citations, and context. New prose must not silently replace source-derived prose.

